-- §21 Which end of the path's factor each end of a carried remainder took, and whether it mattered.
# One row per settled node a remainder is carried up through, with the sign of the figure at each
# end and whether taking corners gave a different figure from multiplying end by end.
#
# Converting a figure that can be negative
#   A demand, a nameplate and a draw cannot be negative, so the smaller factor always lands on the
#   smaller figure, and `composition/converted.sqlc` multiplies end by end. A remainder is a
#   difference and may be negative, so `composition/settled_remainders.sqlc` takes the least and
#   the greatest over both corners instead: more of a shortfall converted at a larger rate is the
#   smaller number. Those are the two branches of one conversion, and this section says which
#   branch each filing actually reached.
#
# `corner_rule_bites` needs both, and that is the point
#   It needs a factor that is a range and a negative figure at that end. Either alone changes
#   nothing. Read the counts here rather than assuming: if both populations are sizeable and the
#   column is false throughout, the two do not overlap in this corpus, and the least and the
#   greatest stand ready for a document nobody has filed.
#
# An empty overlap is a fact about the evidence
#   It is not a reason to simplify the arithmetic. A negative remainder is a layer whose demand
#   exceeds its supply, which is the interference case the model exists to measure, and a factor
#   that is a range is ordinary.
-- composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc, ordered so any biting row reads first.
SELECT c.composition                AS "composition!",
       c.composed_layer             AS "composed_layer!",
       c.node_filing                AS "node_filing!",
       c.node_layer                 AS "node_layer!",
       c.depth                      AS "depth!",
       c.spread_factor              AS "spread_factor!",
       c.operand_low::float8        AS operand_low,
       c.operand_high::float8       AS operand_high,
       c.low_took                   AS "low_took!",
       c.high_took                  AS "high_took!",
       c.corner_rule_bites          AS corner_rule_bites
FROM (
    :compose(composition/conversion_slopes.sqlc)
) c
ORDER BY c.corner_rule_bites DESC NULLS LAST, c.spread_factor DESC, 1, 2, 4
